WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.569
-Our next guests are actors
you know best

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.837
from television's iconic series
"The Sopranos."

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:07.107
Their new book,
"Woke Up This Morning,"

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:09.442
is out now, and new episodes
of their podcast,

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:11.444
"Talking Sopranos,"
are released Mondays

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:13.913
across all podcast platforms
and on YouTube.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.081
Please welcome to the show

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:17.350
Steve Schirripa
and Michael Imperioli.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.352
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:29.362 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:35.601 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.636
Gentlemen, welcome to the show.

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:39.905
-Thanks very having us.
-Thanks for having us, pal.

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:41.641
-You know, there's this
interesting thing happening,

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:42.808
which is, you know, "Sopranos"

00:00:42.808 --> 00:00:44.477
is the most popular show
on television.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.045
It was such a part
of the zeitgeist when it aired,

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:49.816
and now it's having this whole
second moment, it feels like.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.384
Not just your podcast
and the book,

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.519
"Many Saints of Newark"
is out now.

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.721
And I feel like
the one thing people forget

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:56.923
who are watching now is,

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:59.525
it was the biggest deal
in New York City,

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:01.227
this show, when it was on TV.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:05.031
And you guys were, like, the
biggest celebrities in the city.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:07.633
You were -- the "New York Post"
covered you everywhere you went.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:09.335
And I'm wondering,
do the actual wiseguys

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:12.037
ever reach out to you
and tell you what

00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:13.773
they thought about the show?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:18.010
-Well, I lived in Little Italy
on Mulberry Street

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.445
for a couple of seasons, right?

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:21.347
So it was a Monday morning.

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:25.351
The show had aired Sunday night.
And I got my dirty laundry.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.220
I'm going down
to the dry-cleaners.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.256
And I'm walking down the block,
a guy stops me, Rocky.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:32.992
He goes,
"Hey, Steve, Steve, come here.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.428
Look, you know I love the show,
right?

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.697
But anybody
who ever whacked anyone,

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:40.432
that's not how you do it."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:41.935
I said, "Okay, Rock,
I gotta get going."

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:43.403
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:47.440
-I had a similar experience
at Rao's where there was

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.209
an attempted
actual mob hit that --

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:52.811
like, there was a stabbing --
or, a shooting in there.

00:01:52.811 --> 00:01:55.047
I wasn't there that night,
thank God, but --

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:57.416
I was there with Tony Sirico
and Vinny Pastore.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.085
We were at a table,
and the other table

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:01.620
was a captain
in one of the famil--

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.689
I think maybe
the Colombo family.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:04.990
Don't quote me on that.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.829
But Tony knew the guy,
and the guy comes over and said,

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:12.064
"You know, I can show you
the real way

00:02:12.064 --> 00:02:14.133
how to strangle someone
with piano wire."

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:18.237
And luckily, he waited till
dessert to kind of, um...

00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:20.139
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:21.540
...tell us that,
but I thought that was --

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.176
-And then Jim -- didn't Jim
get a crazy call one night?

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:27.179
-Gandolfini got a call
in the middle of the night,

00:02:27.179 --> 00:02:29.616
anonymous call.
He never figured out who it was.

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:31.750
He answers the phone,
and the guy says,

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.520
"Listen,
we like what you're doing.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.457
Doing a really good job,
but know one thing.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:41.094
A don never wears shorts."
Click.

00:02:41.094 --> 00:02:43.663
[ Laughter and applause ]
That was that.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:46.232
That's true.
He never figured out who it --

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.967
-I just love --
I like to think that there's

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.168
a family meeting
where they're like,

00:02:49.168 --> 00:02:50.637
"Somebody's got to make
a phone call."

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.071
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.273
"We can't have people
thinking dons wear shorts."

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:57.476
[ Laughter ]
-"Makes us look bad."

00:02:57.476 --> 00:02:59.678
-"They're gonna give the mob
a bad name."

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:01.181
You know, one of the things
about the show is, you know,

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:03.549
you're all --
you know, you all became,

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:06.485
you know, so well-known
from this show.

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:08.288
And people forget
that a lot of you

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.356
weren't well-known at all
when this show was cast.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.925
I mean, James Gandolfini
was relatively unknown,

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:16.162
and I guess Lorraine Bracco was
maybe the most famous person,

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.263
because she had been
in "Goodfellas."

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:19.498
-She had been nominated
for "Goodfellas,"

00:03:19.498 --> 00:03:21.033
for an Academy Award,
and then --

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:24.069
and Jim had been working,
and Edie Falco,

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:26.272
and they were all working
actors, Michael was working.

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:30.643
But listen, Uncle Junior,
Dominic Chianese?

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.179
69 years old
when he gets the show.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.381
He's sleeping in a La-Z-Boy,

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.716
living in a studio apartment
above a restaurant.

00:03:37.716 --> 00:03:38.951
[ Laughter ]
-Wow.

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:40.853
-Tony Sirico --
Paulie Walnuts --

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:44.857
he's living on his mother's cot,
55 years old.

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:47.092
You know, I had a full-time job.

00:03:47.092 --> 00:03:49.562
I was an entertainment director
in Las Vegas.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.831
And I came here for a wedding.

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:53.499
You know,
I had been acting a little bit.

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:55.802
Came for a wedding,
auditioned,

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:57.870
and I got the job.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.241
And the first year, I had to fly
myself out, put myself up.

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:05.945
It cost me $24,000
to make $22,000.

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:08.214
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.682
-Your agent chipped in 5%
of the difference, though.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.083
-Yeah, yeah, my agent told me --

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:14.153
Believe it or not, my agent
said, "Don't take the job.

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.088
They're not gonna
take care of you."

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:18.091
He is no longer my agent,
obviously.

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:19.926
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.161
-You guys, you know,
one of the reasons behind

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:24.563
starting the podcast was,
it started to occur to you,

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:27.000
based on interactions,
that people were re-engaging

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.467
with the show
all these years later,

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.903
that you had fans that maybe
weren't even born yet

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.271
or were certainly too young
for the show

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:33.572
when it first started airing.

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:37.377
-Yeah, in 2019, I was shooting
a pilot in Central Park.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:41.347
And this kid walks up to me
from Scotland, he was like 20,

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:42.648
and he lifts up his pant leg,

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:45.785
and there's a tattoo of me
on his calf.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:46.952
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:50.523
I was like, "What?
What is that about?"

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:53.292
And, apparently,
there's a lot of people

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:55.327
that have gotten "Soprano"
tattoos on their body --

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:58.731
-How many people have a tattoo
of Michael Imperioli?

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:02.268
[ Laughter ]
Anybody?

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:05.505
-And then, you know,
just like two days ago,

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:08.341
I got tagged on Instagram
from tons of people

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:13.211
dressed up as me
and Adriana for Halloween,

00:05:13.211 --> 00:05:14.513
which was pretty wild.

00:05:14.513 --> 00:05:17.249
All different types of people,
all different sizes.

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:19.084
-Yeah.
-Ethnicity.

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:21.454
And, you know, nobody's shy
about being a Soprano.

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:25.424
-And so the podcast almost got
derailed by the pandemic, right?

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:27.660
The original plans of getting
the two of you guys together.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.163
-Yeah, you know, the reason
we started the podcast --

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.297
So, we got approached to do one.

00:05:32.297 --> 00:05:36.068
We knew all these younger people
were watching the show,

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:40.640
and there was a lot of "Soprano"
podcasts out there with --

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.908
from people who had nothing
to do with the show.

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:44.743
So we're going, "Well,
what the hell do they know?"

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:45.912
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.013
"Let's do a podcast.
We were there, right?"

00:05:48.013 --> 00:05:52.151
So we did all this press,
and we were getting ready

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.253
to start,
and then the pandemic hit.

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:58.991
And then I said, like, "I mean,
who really gives a [bleep]

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:01.226
about a TV show?" you know?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.226 --> 00:06:03.696
The world's coming to an end,
and we're going to --

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:07.233
Who cares about Paulie Walnuts,
right? So...

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:09.235
And then,
we started hearing from people.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:10.569
You know,
"We're waiting for this.

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:12.938
We're bingeing.
We're locked in the house

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:16.609
23 hours a day," as we were.
Depressed as hell.

00:06:16.609 --> 00:06:19.212
And we found Zoom somehow,
and...

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:20.513
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:21.781
...we did it.
-Yeah.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:23.949
That was another rule of the mob
was never use Zoom.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:25.518
-Don't use Zoom.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:28.654
-Never wear shorts,
don't ever do it on Zoom.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.823
And, you guys, one of the things
you do on the podcast,

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:32.725
you interview people
who were on the show.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:35.127
And you've also been
rewatching it.

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:38.064
Do you have unique memories
of watching it as it aired?

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:39.432
Was that something you'd do?

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:41.400
Would you watch it on Sunday
night with the rest of us?

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.635
-I would watch it, yeah,
with my wife on a Sunday night

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:45.905
if I was home, you know?

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.840
The only time
we watched it together,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.644
we were in Florida
at the Hard Rock for the finale.

00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:53.546
Right?
And it was nine of us.

00:06:53.546 --> 00:06:56.081
-The main cast.
-Yeah, the main cast,

00:06:56.081 --> 00:06:58.250
nine of us.
Drank champagne.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.320
And even though I knew
it was gonna fade to black,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.423
I was, like, stunned.
I had -- I hated it.

00:07:04.423 --> 00:07:07.259
I said, "What the hell is this?"
And Michael loved it.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:09.128
Jim didn't know what the hell
was going on.

00:07:09.128 --> 00:07:10.963
Nobody knew what was going on.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:14.100
And that was the only time
that we watched it together.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.002
Otherwise, just watched it
on Sundays, you know?

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:20.105
-That night I was -- I did
an improv show that night.

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:21.640
It was my job to tape it,

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.609
and then everybody
came back to my apartment.

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:26.612
And when it went to black,
every single person looked at me

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.614
thinking that I had not
taped it long enough.

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:30.682
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:33.051
And, like, the greatest relief
of my life

00:07:33.051 --> 00:07:34.720
was when the first credit
came up on screen.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.923
I'm like, "Son -- Oh, my God."
-Everybody.

00:07:37.923 --> 00:07:39.291
-You know,
there's a really good story

00:07:39.291 --> 00:07:40.993
that involves this building,
kind of,

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:43.295
connected to "The Sopranos."
About 20 years ago,

00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:46.232
some nut sent anthrax
to 30 Rockefeller Center.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:48.200
-Yeah, I was working here.
-Okay.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.669
So, Tony Sirico,
played Paulie Walnuts,

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:53.505
figures he's next on the list,

00:07:53.505 --> 00:07:55.641
'cause if you want to bring down
Western civilization,

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:57.943
you got Brian Williams
and then Paulie Walnuts.

00:07:57.943 --> 00:07:59.311
-Totally.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:03.015
-So he figures out --
he gets his assistant

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:05.717
to go to the mailbox
with rubber gloves,

00:08:05.717 --> 00:08:06.819
brings him in the house,

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.121
and puts his mail
in the microwave,

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:11.823
and zaps it for like 30 minutes,
figuring somehow...

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:13.692
-That will stop the anthrax.
-That'll neutralize it.

00:08:13.692 --> 00:08:15.761
I didn't -- I never knew
that that was effective.

00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:17.062
-I don't think it is.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.062 --> 00:08:20.633
-Well, what it did do,
the mail went up in flames.

00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:23.335
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.335 --> 00:08:25.370
But he didn't get anthrax'd.
He survived.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.940
-So they know, Paulie Walnuts
is the guy with the wings.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.142
-Yeah.
-And him and Grandpa Munster

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:32.311
are the only two people
in the world with that hairdo.

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:33.512
[ Laughter ]

00:08:33.512 --> 00:08:36.181
It never took off
like the Elvis or the Rachel.

00:08:36.181 --> 00:08:39.385
-No, no, nobody was getting --
nobody went in to their stylist

00:08:39.385 --> 00:08:40.686
and was like,
"Give me a Paulie," yeah.

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:41.887
[ Laughter ]

00:08:41.887 --> 00:08:44.090
Were you guys -- because,
you know, part of the joy

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.259
of the podcast is the two of you
obviously get along very well.

00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:48.727
You have a very nice rapport.

00:08:48.727 --> 00:08:50.596
Were you fast friends
when you first met on the show?

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:51.730
Did you get along right away?

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.399
-We're not fast friends now.
-Okay.

00:08:53.399 --> 00:08:55.200
[ Laughter ]
-We were friends for a while,

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.969
and then the podcast
kind of ended that.

00:08:56.969 --> 00:08:59.071
-Kind of went like this.
Whoa!

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:02.073
-Yeah, sure.
Flattened the curve, yeah.

00:09:02.073 --> 00:09:04.176
-Listen, Seth, I've known you
quite a while now, right?

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:06.778
-Yeah.
-First day on the set,

00:09:06.778 --> 00:09:09.014
my first day, I'm excited.

00:09:09.014 --> 00:09:11.483
First day, I wasn't even
a full-time actor.

00:09:11.483 --> 00:09:15.153
They say, "Steve,
meet Michael Imperioli."

00:09:15.153 --> 00:09:16.955
He gave me the fish hand.

00:09:16.955 --> 00:09:19.091
You know, like,
the "pay no mind list."

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:20.592
Like he went,
"Yeah, how you doing?

00:09:20.592 --> 00:09:22.361
Yeah, how you doing?"
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.361 --> 00:09:23.929
-You just said you weren't
a full-time actor.

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:25.765
How am I supposed to treat you?

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:28.534
-The guy's aloof.
He hi-hatted me.

00:09:28.534 --> 00:09:30.569
He big-timed me, you know?
-Yep.

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:32.471
-And he's still doing it
to this day, I'll be honest.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:34.006
-He was showing off,
handing out --

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:35.875
-Nothing has changed!
-He was handing out

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:37.309
business cards from the Riviera,
he goes,

00:09:37.309 --> 00:09:39.311
"I'm the V.P. of the Riviera."
[ Laughter ]

00:09:39.311 --> 00:09:42.148
He claimed to have invented
pai gow poker,

00:09:42.148 --> 00:09:43.582
which I didn't believe.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:43.582 --> 00:09:45.350
He's -- you know,
he's full of [bleep]

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.619
-This is the deal.
Michael's a Buddhist.

00:09:47.619 --> 00:09:50.122
He meditates.
He doesn't eat meat.

00:09:50.122 --> 00:09:53.192
He doesn't drink.
I do everything opposite.

00:09:53.192 --> 00:09:54.626
[ Laughter ]

00:09:54.626 --> 00:09:57.063
Everything he does,
I do opposite.

00:09:57.063 --> 00:10:00.499
On paper, we should not be even
in the same room together.

00:10:00.499 --> 00:10:01.734
[ Laughter ]

00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:04.036
-Well, gentlemen,
it works out really well.

00:10:04.036 --> 00:10:06.538
I'm so happy about the podcast.
I'm so excited about the book.

00:10:06.538 --> 00:10:07.673
Thanks for making time
for us tonight.

00:10:07.673 --> 00:10:09.041
-Thank you, man.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:09.041 --> 00:10:11.210
-"Woke Up This Morning"
is available

